# ── walkthroughs and the simulation ──────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The first two walkthroughs' frames were deleted on 2026-09-14 and stay deleted: 94MB of
# screenshots of two older versions of the product, which a third pass would have been read
# against. Their WRITTEN record is committed, in docs/walkthrough-archive/.
docs/walkthrough/
docs/walkthrough-2/

# 🔴 THE SIMULATION'S FRAMES ARE COMMITTED, DELIBERATELY, and that is the exception rather
# than a lapse. They are the proof that six months of this product was operated rather than
# described, and they are the raw material for the films. docs/simulation/05-CAPTURE.md
# caps them at roughly 600 and says what to photograph; past that, the capture is
# photographing states rather than people. They land in docs/simulation-run/, which nothing
# below ignores, and that is on purpose: a frame this file quietly swallowed would be a
# missing proof nobody notices until the run is over.
#
# 🔴 C358 — THE OPERATOR'S FRAMES ARE COMMITTED FOR THIS RUN, AND THE RULE DID NOT BEND.
#
# C80 said admin console frames are never committed. Its reason was disclosure: a console
# shows many people at once, and this repository is treated as if it will be public one day,
# so one real name in one frame is something that cannot be recalled.
#
# That reason is about REAL PEOPLE, not about admin screens. This simulation has none. Every
# surname is Demo or Example and every address is at a domain RFC 2606 reserves, which cannot
# reach an inbox and therefore cannot belong to anybody.
#
# So the precondition is PROVED rather than assumed, and the proof is the gate:
#
#     npm run verify:synthetic
#
# It reads every surname and every email column in the schema, found from information_schema
# rather than from a list, and it plants a real-looking name to watch itself catch one. No
# operator frame is committed until it passes, and it is re-run immediately before the commit,
# because a name that arrives after it passed is a name nobody checked.
#
# 🔴 If that check ever fails, restore the two lines below and delete the frames. The right
# way to relax a rule is to prove what it was protecting, and the right way to lose one is to
# keep the relaxation after the proof stops holding.
#
# docs/walkthrough-3/**/op-*/
# docs/walkthrough-3/**/*admin*


# 🔴 The mini simulation's screenshots. The dev branch carries gate residue with
# real-looking surnames, so nothing photographed there is committable:
# `scripts/demo.ts` says what publishing an operator frame would disclose.
.probe-shots/

# 🔴 80.6 — EVERY SCREENSHOT IS EVIDENCE, AND EVIDENCE DOES NOT LIVE IN GIT.
#
# The walkthrough protocol in docs/TAKEOVER.md asks for a screenshot before and
# after every click, from every persona, on every scenario. That is tens of
# images per flow and hundreds per run, and their whole purpose is to be
# stitched into a demo video per feature and then superseded by the next run.
#
# Committing them did exactly what you would expect: this repository carried 35
# PNGs of two older versions of the product, in `captures/` and `docs/screens/`,
# showing screens that no longer exist, next to a README describing a walkthrough
# nobody had run since. A new session reading those would learn the wrong product.
#
# So the tools stay, the output is ignored, and a run's evidence is a directory
# on disk plus the report that references it.
captures/
docs/screens/
evidence/
